{"id": 32, "ts": 1780793492.751819, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_1", "message": "I agree with Agent\u202f3\u2019s conclusion and keep Candidate\u202fB as my provisional recommendation.\u202fThe evidence that sets B apart is his proven stress\u2011resilience (\u201chandles stress very well\u201d) and operational reliability (\u201cvery conscientious\u201d), both non\u2011negotiable for long\u2011haul flights.\u202fHis technical competence is also clear \u2013 he \u201cassesses weather conditions well\u201d and has \u201cexcellent computer skills,\u201d covering the cognitive and systems\u2011knowledge requirements.\nThe interpersonal concerns \u2013 being \u201cgrumpy\u201d and \u201cuncooperative\u201d \u2013 are noted, but we have no concrete incidents showing they impair crew cooperation, whereas Candidate\u202fD\u2019s arrogance, hot temper, and weak leadership are explicitly flagged as likely to undermine cockpit teamwork.\u202fCandidate\u202fA, while showing leadership and situational awareness, is described as unorganized and resistant to criticism, and we lack any stress\u2011resilience evidence for him.\u202fCandidate\u202fC is 100\u202f% reliable and creates a positive crew atmosphere, yet his difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric attitude, and lack of willingness to further his education raise doubts, and we have no data on his performance under pressure.\nGiven the balance of strong, safety\u2011critical strengths versus less\u2011substantiated interpersonal risks, Candidate\u202fB remains the strongest overall fit for the long\u2011distance pilot role."}
